+// ---- cross-block write/read hazard on the folded add chain ------------------
+//
+// rms_norm_pre_add_launch issues one block per row.  Block r writes add_dst
+// row r in loop 1 and dst row r in loop 2, with only the block-scope
+// __syncthreads() inside the block reduction between them, and it reads every
+// add operand at row r in loop 1.  Nothing orders one block's loop 2 against
+// another block's loop 1, so a WRITE range must never intersect a READ range
+// that belongs to a different block.
+//
+// Every writer and reader here is contiguous and the same shape, so their row
+// grids are congruent and an overlapping pair is safe only when the two tensors
+// start at exactly the same address: block r then writes precisely the row
+// block r reads, which is the in-place residual add the kernel already assumes.
+// Any other overlap, INCLUDING one a whole number of rows apart, puts block r's
+// store on top of block r' != r's load.
+//
+// This is a correctness fix carried over from the qwen3.6 r9700 round-51 work
+// (patch 0065), where the same fold is shipped and the hazard was diagnosed:
+// 9 of 79 folds per graph placed the norm output over an add operand with the
+// bases 128 bytes apart - not a whole multiple of the 8192-byte row stride - so
+// block r's loop-2 store landed on the last 32 of 2048 floats of block r-1's
+// loop-1 operand row.  It only fires when the blocks land in different dispatch
+// waves, which is why small row counts are bit-stable and 512 rows are not.
+// Unguarded it moves perplexity by up to 0.107% between loads of one binary.
+static bool ggml_cuda_pre_add_write_safe(const ggml_tensor * w, const ggml_tensor * r) {
+    if (r == nullptr || w->data == r->data) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    return ggml_cuda_mmvq_ranges_disjoint(w, r);
+}
